# A q35-class PC that boots **UEFI out of flash**, the way a machine built this
# century does.
#
#   rsemu run q35-uefi --flash0 OVMF_CODE.fd --flash1 OVMF_VARS.fd
#   rsemu run q35-uefi --flash0 OVMF_CODE.fd --drive flash1=OVMF_VARS.fd
#   rsemu run q35-uefi --flash0 OVMF_CODE.fd --drive flash1=OVMF_VARS.fd \
#       --drive nvme0=esp.img -p disk=64M
#   rsemu run q35-uefi --flash0 edk2-x86_64-code.fd --flash1 edk2-i386-vars.fd \
#       -p flash=4M -p vars=528K --console debug
#
# The last one is the interesting one when a boot goes wrong: this board has
# three character ports — `keyboard`, COM1's `console`, and `debug`, which is
# where EDK II's `PlatformDebugLibIoPort` puts its whole `DEBUG()` log — so a
# terminal has to be told which. An image with debug strings in it narrates the
# boot from the reset vector on that third one.
#
# The second form is the one that makes a variable outlive the run: `--flash1`
# copies bytes in and nothing takes them out again, while `--drive` binds the
# bank to a host **file** through `dev/medium`, and `flash.cfi`'s `flush` writes
# it back when the machine stops.
#
# # What this board is, against `q35`
#
# The same chipset, with the **firmware socket replaced by two banks of
# parallel NOR flash**. `machines/q35.machine` has a `pc.rom` at `0xf0000` and
# an alias of it at the top of the address space, which is a 1996 machine: a
# mask ROM the processor executes and nothing writes. A UEFI machine has flash,
# and the difference is not cosmetic — the variable store is the firmware's own
# non-volatile memory, and a part that can only *clear* bits is what UEFI's
# fault-tolerant write is built on top of (`src/dev/flash/cfi.rs`).
#
# This is the same arrangement `machines/riscv-virt.machine` already boots EDK
# II from, one architecture over: a read-only code bank and a writable variable
# bank, both `flash.cfi`, both filled from a media slot. What is x86-specific is
# only *where they go* and *how wide they are* — see the `map` block and the
# `flash.cfi` objects.
#
# # Where the banks go, and why it is not a choice
#
# An x86 processor fetches its first instruction from `0xfffffff0`, sixteen
# bytes below the top of the address space (Intel SDM Vol. 3A §9.1.4). The code
# bank therefore **ends at 4 GiB**, and the variable bank sits immediately below
# it, so the pair is one contiguous run of flash whose top is the reset vector.
# That is the layout every split OVMF build is compiled for, and it is why
# `vars` and `flash` are sizes rather than addresses: the addresses fall out of
# them.
#
#   0x100000000 - flash          the variable store, `vars` bytes
#   0x100000000 - flash + vars   the firmware, `flash - vars` bytes
#   0x100000000 - 16             the reset vector, at the top of the last block
#
# The defaults describe a **2 MiB** split OVMF: a 1920 KiB code image and a
# 128 KiB variable store. A 4 MiB build is `-p flash=4M -p vars=528K`.
#
# # What is gone from `q35`, and why each one
#
#   * **The `pc.rom` sockets.** Replaced, as above. Nothing maps `0xf0000` any
#     more: on a UEFI machine that band is neither ROM nor a table store, and
#     leaving it open bus is the honest description.
#   * **`q35.acpi`.** The firmware publishes its own ACPI set and hands the
#     operating system an RSDP through the EFI configuration table (UEFI 2.10
#     §4.6). A board that also staged a *generated* RSDP at `0xe0000` would be
#     offering a legacy scan a second, different description of itself — the
#     exact ambiguity `machines/q35.machine` warns about for the MP table, with
#     the roles reversed. One machine, one set of tables, and here they are the
#     firmware's — which is not the same as saying the board is silent about
#     itself. It says the same things through `q35.fwcfg` below, which is where
#     an OVMF build gets the bytes it installs; the difference is *who
#     publishes*, not who knows.
#   * **The video adapter and its option ROM.** EDK II's `QemuVideoDxe` binds
#     three PCI identifications and none of them is ours, so a display adapter
#     on this board would be a card nothing drives. The console is the 16550,
#     which is what EDK II's `PlatformBootManagerLib` puts a terminal on and
#     what every measurement in `docs/platforms/q35-uefi.md` is taken through.
#   * **Both IDE channels and the 8237As.** A UEFI build reaches its disks
#     through a PCI storage controller, not through `0x1f0`; each of these would
#     be a second thing under test. The controller it does reach them through is
#     the `nvme.controller` at `00:04.0` below.
#
# # The disk is on the bus and is reachable
#
# `nvme0` is enumerated by the firmware, bound by EDK II's `NvmExpressDxe`, and
# for a while answered `0xffffffff` at every register, because a base address
# register programmed through the **ECAM** window never decoded. That was a
# defect in `src/bus/pci/bar.rs` rather than anything about this board and it is
# fixed; `tests/q35_uefi.rs` reproduces it in sixty milliseconds with no
# firmware and `docs/platforms/q35-uefi.md` has the mechanism. With it fixed,
# BDS finds a FAT volume on the namespace, starts `\EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI` off
# it, and a Linux kernel entered through its EFI stub comes up on COM1.
#
# # A20 is deliberately unwired
#
# The same decision `machines/q35-linux.machine` documents, for the same reason
# and with a different guest. A net with a driver comes up low, and a low `a20`
# pin shuts the gate; the firmware on `q35` opens it during POST. UEFI firmware
# does not: its reset vector goes from real mode to long mode without ever
# touching `0x92` or the 8042's output port, because the machines it is written
# for come out of reset with A20 already connected. So the 8042 is here — a
# `Ps2KeyboardDxe` that found open bus at `0x64` would spend its whole timeout
# there — but its `a20` pin drives nothing, and an unwired pin is a permanently
# connected address line.
#
# # Sources
#
# Intel 3 Series Express Chipset Family Datasheet (316966-002) for the (G)MCH;
# Intel I/O Controller Hub 9 Family Datasheet (316972-004) for the south bridge;
# Intel SDM Vol. 3A §9.1.4 for the reset state; the UEFI Specification 2.10 and
# the PI Specification 1.8 for what a firmware expects of a platform; JEDEC
# JESD68.01 and the Intel StrataFlash P30 datasheet for the flash. EDK II itself
# (BSD-2-Clause-Patent, readable under `ROADMAP.md` §1) for which of those flash
# commands `OvmfPkg`'s driver actually issues.
#
# **No emulator source was consulted** (ROADMAP.md §1).

machine "q35-uefi" {
  # -- parameters -------------------------------------------------------------

  # Base memory: the 640 KiB below the video hole.
  param basemem = 640K

  # Extended memory, from 0x100000 up. UEFI is not a boot sector: the DXE core
  # allocates a heap, decompresses a firmware volume into it and keeps every
  # driver it dispatched resident, so 128 MiB is a floor rather than a generous
  # number.
  #
  # It is also the number the firmware *reads*: `pc.rtc` publishes it in CMOS
  # `0x34`/`0x35` as 64 KiB blocks above 16 MiB, which is the pair EDK II's
  # `PlatformInitLib` sizes memory from. It stays the route even now that this
  # board has a `fw_cfg`: `PlatformGetSystemMemorySizeBelow4gb` reaches for an
  # `etc/e820` file first and falls back to CMOS when there is none, and the
  # `q35.fwcfg` object below deliberately serves no such file — one description
  # of how much memory the board has, in the place that already had it.
  # `extmem = 128M` puts the top of low memory at `0x8100000`, and the firmware
  # computes exactly that.
  param extmem = 128M

  # How much flash the board has in total, and how much of it is the variable
  # store. Two sizes rather than two addresses because the addresses are fixed
  # by the reset vector — see the header.
  param flash = 2M
  param vars  = 128K

  # The erase-block size both banks are divided into. 4 KiB, which is what a
  # split OVMF build is laid out in: its variable store, its fault-tolerant
  # working block and that block's spare are each a whole number of these, and
  # an erase that took more would destroy a neighbour.
  param block = 4K

  # Where `PCIEXBAR` comes out of reset pointing, with its enable bit set.
  # Unlike on `q35` this is *not* purely a stand-in for firmware: EDK II's
  # `PlatformPei` programs the register itself, to `PcdPciExpressBaseAddress`,
  # which for a q35 is this address. The board pre-programming it means the
  # window decodes before the firmware gets there and the firmware's own write
  # is a no-op — so both orders work and neither is load bearing.
  param ecambase = 0xe0000000

  # Where `PMBASE` comes out of reset pointing, with `ACPI_EN` set. This one
  # matters more than it looks: EDK II reads the ACPI power-management timer at
  # `PcdAcpiPmBaseAddress + 8` for every `MicroSecondDelay`, and its
  # `AcpiInitialization` only programs `PMBASE` when it finds `ACPI_EN` clear.
  # 0x600 is both the arrangement `machines/q35.machine` uses and the address
  # OVMF is built for, so the firmware finds the timer already running.
  param pmbase = 0x600

  # How big the NVMe namespace is when the run supplies no image. A `--drive
  # nvme0=disk.img`, or bytes in the `nvme0` media slot, brings its own size and
  # this has to be at least as large: 16 MiB is a namespace the firmware
  # enumerates and finds nothing on, which is the right answer for a board
  # booted with no disk at all.
  param disk = 16M

  # Bytes in a logical block. 512 is what an EFI System Partition is laid out
  # in and what EDK II's `NvmExpressDxe` reports up as the media's block size,
  # so a FAT whose BPB says 512 and a namespace that says 4096 would disagree
  # about where every cluster is. Spelled `sector` rather than `block` because
  # `block` on this board is already the flash's *erase* block, and the two are
  # different sizes for different parts.
  param sector = 512

  # Which host character port the keyboard's scan codes arrive on.
  param keyboard = "keyboard"

  # And which one COM1 is, which is this board's only console.
  param console = "console"

  # And which one the firmware's own `DEBUG()` log arrives on. A **second**
  # stream rather than the console's, deliberately: the firmware writes both at
  # once, and a debug log spliced into a UEFI shell's line editor would ruin
  # both. Watch it with `rsemu run q35-uefi --console debug`, or redirect it.
  param debugport = "debug"

  # The date and time the RTC starts at. A device may never read the host clock
  # (CLAUDE.md, determinism), so it is declared.
  param time = "2026-01-01T00:00:00"

  # Which execution engine the processor runs on: `interp`, `jit`, or
  # `jit-host`.
  #
  #   rsemu run q35-uefi -p engine=jit
  #
  # A parameter rather than a fixed property for the reason
  # `machines/riscv-virt.machine` gives at length: the three are supposed to
  # be *indistinguishable to the guest* — same registers, same memory, same
  # cycle counts — and the only way to keep that claim honest is for one
  # board to run all three and have the state hashes compared. `tests/q35_uefi.rs`
  # does that; `docs/platforms/q35-uefi.md` has what each one costs.
  #
  # `interp` is the default because it is the oracle. Both others need a
  # build with `cpu-x86-lift` and `jit`, and a build without them refuses
  # the property with a message rather than quietly interpreting.
  param engine = "interp"

  # -- crystals ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `q35`'s, minus the dot clock a board with no video adapter has no use for.

  osc cpu   = 25000000 Hz
  osc pit   = 105000000/88 Hz
  osc rtc   = 32768 Hz
  osc kbd   = 12000000 Hz
  osc bus   = 100000000 Hz
  osc hpet  = 10000000 Hz
  osc pmtmr = 315000000/88 Hz
  osc uart  = 1843200 Hz

  # -- address spaces ---------------------------------------------------------

  space mem  { width = 64, unassigned = read-as-ones }
  space port { width = 16, unassigned = read-as-ones }

  # -- the processor ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `variant = "x86-64"`, and on this board that is not a preference. A UEFI
  # firmware's SEC phase leaves real mode within a few dozen instructions and is
  # in **long mode** before it calls a single PEIM; everything after the reset
  # vector is 64-bit code with paging on.
  object cpu0 "cpu.x86" {
    clock   = cpu
    space   = mem
    iospace = "port"
    variant = "x86-64"
    engine  = engine
  }

  # -- memory -----------------------------------------------------------------

  object ram_low  "ram" { size = basemem }
  object ram_high "ram" { size = extmem }

  # -- the north bridge -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `machines/q35.machine`'s, unchanged, and its comment block is the long form.
  # `device-id = 0x29c0` is the 82G33/82P35 identification the datasheet prints
  # as the register file's own default — and EDK II's `PlatformInitLib`
  # switches on exactly this value to decide it is looking at a q35 rather than
  # an i440fx, so a board that answered anything else would be initialised as
  # the wrong chipset.
  object mch "q35.mch" {
    device-id   = 0x29c0
    clock       = bus
    space       = mem
    bus         = "pci0"
    ecam        = ecambase
    passthrough = sysctl
  }

  # -- the south bridge -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `machines/q35.machine`'s, unchanged.
  object lpc "q35.lpc" {
    clock       = pmtmr
    bus         = "pci0"
    iospace     = "port"
    device-id   = 0x2918
    pm-base     = pmbase
    pirq-routes = [11, 10, 11, 10]
  }

  # -- interrupts -------------------------------------------------------------

  object pic1   "pc.pic"    { mode = "master" }
  object pic2   "pc.pic"    { mode = "slave" }
  object lapic0 "pc.lapic"  { clock = bus, id = 0, bus = "apic" }
  object ioapic "pc.ioapic" { id = 1, bus = "apic" }
  object imcr   "pc.imcr"   {}

  # -- the timers -------------------------------------------------------------

  object pit0 "pc.pit" { clock = pit }

  object hpet0 "pc.hpet" {
    clock  = hpet
    period = 100000000
    route0 = 16
    route1 = 17
    route2 = 18
  }

  # -- the legacy replacement route -------------------------------------------
  #
  # The board-level multiplexer `LEG_RT_CNF` is (*IA-PC HPET Specification*
  # §2.3.5): while the bit is set, the HPET's comparators 0 and 1 take over
  # IRQ0 and IRQ8 from the 8254 and the RTC, and stop driving the inputs their
  # own route fields name. `machines/q35-linux.machine` has the long form of
  # why it is five gates rather than three; this board needs exactly the same
  # thing for exactly the same reason, and it needed it the moment this machine
  # file gained a `fw_cfg`.
  #
  # **That order is the whole story.** With no ACPI at all, a UEFI-booted kernel
  # never learned this board had an HPET, so `hpet_enable()` failed, Linux
  # programmed the 8254 and took its tick from counter 0 — and a board with the
  # 8254 hard-wired to I/O APIC input 2 was telling the truth. With the HPET
  # table in the set the firmware installs, `hpet_time_init()` succeeds, sets
  # `LEG_RT_CNF`, stops loading counter 0 and waits for comparator 0 on IRQ0 —
  # which on a board without these gates arrives nowhere:
  #
  #   ..MP-BIOS bug: 8254 timer not connected to IO-APIC
  #   Kernel panic - not syncing: IO-APIC + timer doesn't work!
  #
  # measured on this board, between describing it to the firmware and wiring
  # the multiplexer that description implies.
  object legoff "wire.not" {}

  # The two chips the HPET displaces, each held on its line only while the bit
  # is clear.
  object pitgate "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object rtcgate "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # The two comparators that take those lines over while it is set.
  object hpetir0 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object hpetir8 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # And comparators 0 and 1 on their own routes, which they abandon while it is
  # set. Comparator 2 keeps its route unconditionally and needs no gate.
  object hpetr0 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }
  object hpetr1 "wire.and" { inputs = 2 }

  # -- the real-time clock and CMOS -------------------------------------------

  object cmos "pc.rtc" {
    clock   = rtc
    time    = time
    basemem = basemem
    extmem  = extmem
  }

  # -- the rest of the legacy set ---------------------------------------------

  object kbc    "pc.kbc"    { clock = kbd / 12000, port = keyboard }
  object sysctl "pc.sysctl" {}

  # -- the serial port --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # COM1 at 0x3f8 on IRQ4, and this board's only console: EDK II's
  # `PlatformBootManagerLib` puts a terminal on the ISA serial port, and the
  # UEFI shell talks through it.
  object com1 "uart.ns16550" {
    clock     = uart / 16
    port      = console
    frequency = 1843200
  }

  # -- somewhere for the firmware to talk -------------------------------------
  #
  # The hypervisor debug console at 0x402: one byte-wide port that answers 0xe9
  # to a read and takes a character on every write. EDK II's
  # `PlatformDebugLibIoPort` reads it once (`PlatformDebugLibIoPortDetect`),
  # compares the byte against `BOCHS_DEBUG_PORT_MAGIC`, and writes its whole
  # `DEBUG()` log there for the rest of the boot if it matched — and drops every
  # byte of it if it did not. This board's I/O space is `read-as-ones`, so
  # before this object it did not match, and `docs/platforms/q35-uefi.md`
  # records what that cost: a variable-store defect that had to be inferred from
  # four bus cycles when `QemuFlashDetected` prints its own answer on this port.
  #
  # 0x402 rather than anything else because that is `PcdDebugIoPort`'s default
  # in `OvmfPkg/OvmfPkg.dec` — a build-time token, so it is the firmware's
  # choice and belongs here rather than in the device.
  #
  # A `RELEASE` firmware writes nothing here at all: `DEBUG()` is compiled out.
  # The port still answers, and the board is still telling the truth.
  object debugcon "pc.debugcon" { port = debugport }

  # -- what the board tells the firmware about itself -------------------------
  #
  # `fw_cfg`: a selector at 0x510 and a data port at 0x511, and behind them the
  # **ACPI tables**, generated from this realized machine by the same code
  # `machines/q35.machine` hands an operating system directly (`src/dev/q35`).
  #
  # This is the one thing a UEFI board cannot do the way `q35` does. There, a
  # `q35.acpi` device stages an RSDP at 0xe0000 and an operating system finds it
  # by searching (ACPI §5.2.5.1). Under UEFI nothing searches: the kernel takes
  # its RSDP from the EFI configuration table (UEFI 2.10 §4.6), which holds
  # whatever the *firmware* installed — and an OVMF build has exactly one source
  # for that, which is this register pair. A board that stays silent here is a
  # board whose kernel prints `ACPI MADT or MP tables are not detected` and
  # falls back to virtual wire mode, which is what this one used to do.
  #
  # `space = mem` and `iospace = "port"` are how it reads the machine rather
  # than restating it: the local APIC's page, the I/O APIC's, the HPET's, the
  # ECAM window and the ACPI register block are all found by walking those two
  # address spaces, and `bus = "pci0"` is the fabric whose interrupt routing
  # becomes `_PRT`. Nothing here is written down twice.
  #
  # The set it hands over is deliberately small — the tables, the RSDP and the
  # loader script that places them, and no `etc/e820`, no `etc/system-states`,
  # no SMBIOS. Each of those is a lookup EDK II is written to survive, and the
  # one that matters is memory sizing: with no `etc/e820` the firmware keeps
  # reading CMOS 0x34/0x35, which is what `extmem` above already publishes and
  # what every measurement on this board was taken through.
  object fwcfg "q35.fwcfg" {
    space   = mem
    iospace = "port"
    bus     = "pci0"
  }

  # -- the disk ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # An NVM Express controller at device 4, function 0 — the same part at the
  # same address as `machines/q35-linux.machine`, and here for a different
  # reason. There a kernel needs a root; here the **firmware** needs somewhere
  # to look for an operating system. UEFI's boot manager searches the block
  # devices it can see for a FAT file system and, failing a `Boot####` that
  # names something else, for the architecture's default file name
  # `\EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI` (UEFI 2.10 §3.5.1.1, "Removable Media Boot
  # Behavior"). A board with no storage controller has nothing for it to
  # search, which is why this one used to answer `map: No mapping found.`
  #
  # NVMe rather than AHCI or IDE because of what OVMF dispatches: EDK II's
  # `NvmExpressDxe` is a generic `MdeModulePkg` driver that binds on a PCI
  # class code of 01/08/02 and needs nothing from the platform, and it **polls**
  # its completion queues rather than taking an interrupt — so a namespace is
  # reachable with nothing on this board wired for it. That is the same
  # argument `docs/platforms/q35-linux.md` makes for a kernel's built-in driver
  # set, one layer down.
  #
  # `space = mem` is not decoration: the controller is a **bus master**, and its
  # queues, its PRP lists and its data buffers are all in this board's RAM,
  # which it reads and writes itself. Its register block is deliberately absent
  # from the memory map below — `BAR0` places it, and on this board the thing
  # that programs `BAR0` is `PciBusDxe`.
  object nvme0 "nvme.controller" {
    space  = mem
    bus    = "pci0"
    device = 4
    image  = "nvme0"
    size   = disk
    block  = sector
  }

  # -- the flash --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two banks of parallel NOR, which is what makes a UEFI build able to *keep* a
  # variable rather than merely read one. RAM at these addresses boots just as
  # far and then cannot install `gEfiVariableWriteArchProtocolGuid`, because
  # fault-tolerant write needs erase-and-program semantics memory has not got.
  #
  # `width = 1, interleave = 1` is the x86-specific half, and it is not a
  # simplification. `machines/riscv-virt.machine` wires two x16 parts on a
  # 32-bit bus because that is what its firmware's driver assumes; EDK II's
  # `OvmfPkg` flash driver is written against a **byte-wide** part and issues
  # every command as a single byte store, so a device whose command word was two
  # bytes would reject each one as a misaligned access. One x8 part it is.
  #
  # `locked = false` is the other one. An Intel P30 powers up with every block
  # locked and wants a `0x60`/`0xd0` unlock before a program or an erase; the
  # `OvmfPkg` driver never issues one, so a board that came up locked would
  # answer every variable write with SR.1 set. `machines/riscv-virt.machine`
  # leaves the default alone because `VirtNorFlashDxe` *does* unlock.
  #
  # `flash0` is `readonly`: the bank a board boots from has `WP#` tied low, and
  # firmware that is not meant to be guest-updatable should be refused rather
  # than silently corrupted. `flash1` is not, and is the whole point — bind it
  # with `--drive flash1=vars.fd` and what the guest programs is in the file
  # when the run ends.
  #
  # Nothing here says whether the firmware will *use* the variable bank. That
  # is decided four bus cycles into DXE, by whether the part's status register
  # reads zero after a Clear Status Register — `flash.cfi`'s business, not the
  # board's, and `docs/platforms/q35-uefi.md` has the boot where it did not.
  object flashcode "flash.cfi" {
    size       = flash - vars
    image      = "flash0"
    block      = block
    width      = 1
    interleave = 1
    locked     = false
    readonly   = true
  }

  object flashvars "flash.cfi" {
    size       = vars
    image      = "flash1"
    block      = block
    width      = 1
    interleave = 1
    locked     = false
  }

  # -- the memory map ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 0x00000     base RAM, up to the video hole
  # 0x100000    extended memory
  # 0xfec00000  the I/O APIC, 0xfed00000 the HPET, 0xfee00000 the local APIC
  # 4G - flash  the variable store, then the firmware, then the reset vector
  #
  # The ECAM window is **not** here: `PCIEXBAR` places it.
  map mem 0x00000000 size basemem = ram_low
  map mem 0x00100000 size extmem  = ram_high

  map mem 0xfec00000 size 0x0020 = ioapic.regs   # 82093AA §3.1
  map mem 0xfed00000 size 0x0400 = hpet0.regs    # HPET spec §3.2.4
  map mem 0xfee00000 size 0x1000 = lapic0.regs   # SDM Vol 3A §10.4.4

  map mem 0x100000000 - flash        size vars         = flashvars
  map mem 0x100000000 - flash + vars size flash - vars = flashcode

  # -- the I/O map ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The ACPI register block is not here: `PMBASE` places it.
  map port 0x0020 size 0x0002 = pic1.regs
  map port 0x0022 size 0x0002 = imcr.regs
  map port 0x0040 size 0x0004 = pit0.regs
  map port 0x0060 size 0x0001 = kbc.data
  map port 0x0061 size 0x0001 = sysctl.portb
  map port 0x0064 size 0x0001 = kbc.cmd
  map port 0x0070 size 0x0002 = cmos.regs
  map port 0x0092 size 0x0001 = sysctl.porta
  map port 0x00a0 size 0x0002 = pic2.regs
  map port 0x03f8 size 0x0008 = com1.regs
  map port 0x0402 size 0x0001 = debugcon.regs # OvmfPkg.dec: PcdDebugIoPort
  map port 0x04d0 size 0x0001 = pic1.elcr
  map port 0x04d1 size 0x0001 = pic2.elcr
  map port 0x0510 size 0x0002 = fwcfg.regs   # selector and data; no DMA at 0x514
  map port 0x0cf8 size 0x0008 = mch.config

  # -- the wires --------------------------------------------------------------

  wire pic2.int    -> pic1.ir2
  wire pic1.int    -> imcr.int
  wire imcr.intr   -> cpu0.intr
  wire imcr.lint0  -> lapic0.lint0
  wire lapic0.intr -> cpu0.intr
  wire lapic0.nmi  -> cpu0.nmi

  wire kbc.irq1    -> pic1.ir1
  wire kbc.irq1    -> ioapic.irq1
  wire kbc.irq12   -> pic2.ir4
  wire kbc.irq12   -> ioapic.irq12

  wire com1.irq    -> pic1.ir4
  wire com1.irq    -> ioapic.irq4

  # IRQ0 and IRQ8, through the multiplexer the HPET's `LEG_RT_CNF` is — see the
  # object block above. `legoff` inverts the bit, so its output is high exactly
  # while the 8254 and the RTC own the two lines.
  #
  # IRQ0 is the one line whose two halves do not carry the same number: it
  # reaches I/O APIC input **2** rather than 0, whichever chip is driving it,
  # and the MADT's interrupt source override — which `fwcfg` above hands the
  # firmware — is the other half of that.
  wire hpet0.legacy -> legoff.in

  wire legoff.out   -> pitgate.in0
  wire pit0.out0    -> pitgate.in1
  wire pitgate.out  -> pic1.ir0
  wire pitgate.out  -> ioapic.irq2

  wire legoff.out   -> rtcgate.in0
  wire cmos.irq     -> rtcgate.in1
  wire rtcgate.out  -> pic2.ir0
  wire rtcgate.out  -> ioapic.irq8

  # The other half of each multiplexer: two sources on one net is the
  # wired-OR `ROADMAP.md` §4.3 describes, and it is the right shape here — at
  # most one side is ever enabled, so the resolve never has to arbitrate.
  wire hpet0.legacy -> hpetir0.in0
  wire hpet0.t0     -> hpetir0.in1
  wire hpetir0.out  -> pic1.ir0
  wire hpetir0.out  -> ioapic.irq2

  wire hpet0.legacy -> hpetir8.in0
  wire hpet0.t1     -> hpetir8.in1
  wire hpetir8.out  -> pic2.ir0
  wire hpetir8.out  -> ioapic.irq8

  # Comparators 0 and 1 on the inputs their route fields name, which they hold
  # only while the legacy replacement route is off. Comparator 2 is never
  # displaced, so it is wired straight through.
  wire legoff.out   -> hpetr0.in0
  wire hpet0.t0     -> hpetr0.in1
  wire hpetr0.out   -> ioapic.irq16

  wire legoff.out   -> hpetr1.in0
  wire hpet0.t1     -> hpetr1.in1
  wire hpetr1.out   -> ioapic.irq17

  wire hpet0.t2     -> ioapic.irq18

  wire lpc.sci9    -> pic2.ir1
  wire lpc.sci9    -> ioapic.irq9
  wire lpc.sci10   -> pic2.ir2
  wire lpc.sci10   -> ioapic.irq10
  wire lpc.sci11   -> pic2.ir3
  wire lpc.sci11   -> ioapic.irq11
  wire lpc.sci20   -> ioapic.irq20
  wire lpc.sci21   -> ioapic.irq21

  wire lpc.irq3    -> pic1.ir3
  wire lpc.irq3    -> ioapic.irq3
  wire lpc.irq4    -> pic1.ir4
  wire lpc.irq4    -> ioapic.irq4
  wire lpc.irq5    -> pic1.ir5
  wire lpc.irq5    -> ioapic.irq5
  wire lpc.irq6    -> pic1.ir6
  wire lpc.irq6    -> ioapic.irq6
  wire lpc.irq7    -> pic1.ir7
  wire lpc.irq7    -> ioapic.irq7
  wire lpc.irq10   -> pic2.ir2
  wire lpc.irq10   -> ioapic.irq10
  wire lpc.irq11   -> pic2.ir3
  wire lpc.irq11   -> ioapic.irq11
  wire lpc.irq12   -> pic2.ir4
  wire lpc.irq12   -> ioapic.irq12
  wire lpc.irq14   -> pic2.ir6
  wire lpc.irq14   -> ioapic.irq14
  wire lpc.irq15   -> pic2.ir7
  wire lpc.irq15   -> ioapic.irq15

  wire sysctl.gate2 -> pit0.gate2
  wire pit0.out2    -> sysctl.timer2
  wire pit0.out1    -> sysctl.refresh

  # Reset, from both the 8042's pulse and `0xcf9`. **A20 is not wired** — see
  # the header. `kbc.a20` and `sysctl.a20` exist and drive nothing, so the
  # address line stays connected however a guest programs the two chips.
  wire kbc.reset    -> cpu0.reset
  wire sysctl.reset -> cpu0.reset
}
